arXiv:2509.03345v1 [cs.AI] 03 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: 
<br class="ltx_break">A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yunxin Sun &amp; Abulhair Saparov
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{sun1114, asaparov}@purdue.edu</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Purdue University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>West Lafayette, IN 47907
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning is a core capability in artificial intelligence systems, for which large language models (LLMs) have recently shown remarkable progress. <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">However</em>, most work focuses exclusively on deductive reasoning, which is problematic since other types of reasoning are also essential in solving real-world problems, and they are less explored. This work focuses on evaluating LLMs’ inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities. We introduce a programmable and synthetic dataset, 
<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InAbHyD</span>
(pronounced <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">in-a-bid</span>), where each reasoning example consists of an incomplete world model and a set of observations. The task for the intelligent agent is to produce hypotheses to <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explain</em> observations under the incomplete world model to solve each reasoning example. We propose a new metric to evaluate the quality of hypotheses based on Occam’s Razor. We evaluate and analyze some state-of-the-art LLMs. Our analysis shows that LLMs can perform inductive and abductive reasoning in simple scenarios, but struggle with complex world models and producing <em id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-quality</em> hypotheses, even with popular reasoning-enhancing techniques such as in-context learning and RLVR. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        All data and generation code are freely available at <a href="https://github.com/byrantwithyou/inabhyd/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/byrantwithyou/inabhyd/</a>.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning is a hallmark of human intelligence. Peirce categorizes reasoning into deductive, inductive, and abductive reasoning  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burch, 2024</a>)</cite>. Deductive reasoning draws a specific conclusion from a set of premises. For example, given “All humans are moral” and “Socrates is a human”, we can conclude that “Socrates is moral”. Inductive reasoning aims to identify a general principle from multiple individual cases. For example, if we see many cute cats, we may inductively reason that all cats are cute. Abductive reasoning seeks to produce hypotheses to explain observations. For example, if we hear a bird sound outside, we may abductively reason that there is a bird. The goal in both types of reasoning is to find explanations to explain observations (inductive reasoning focuses on inducing a more general rule, while abductive reasoning focuses on finding a ground fact or axiom).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The composition of inductive and abductive reasoning is ubiquitous in real-world scenarios. For example, given (i) <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Amy has a skin rash and leg pain and has been diagnosed with a new unknown virus.</span> (ii) <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sam has a skin rash and leg pain and has been diagnosed with the same new unknown virus.</span> (iii) <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tom also has a skin rash and leg pain.</span> We can first induce the rule: <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">The new unknown virus causes skin rashes and leg pain</span>, and then we can abduce that <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tom is infected with this new virus</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental difference between deductive and inductive or abductive reasoning is that deductive reasoning is deterministic. The conclusion in deductive reasoning is deterministically correct if the proof is sound and the premises are correct.
However, inductive and abductive reasoning are inherently probabilistic. Technically, there may exist many conclusions, some <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">more probable (high-quality)</em> and some <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">less probable (low-quality)</em>. For example, in the above bird sound case, a <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">less probable</em> conclusion is that someone outside is playing the bird sound on a speaker. In this regard, we view inductive and abductive reasoning as the inverse processes of deductive reasoning and study them through a unified lens. In many real-world scenarios, all three reasoning modalities are indispensable. Deductive reasoning is widely used in mathematical problem-solving, inductive reasoning is commonly used in scientific research and discovery, and abductive reasoning is often employed in medical diagnosis. Knowing and applying the principles of parsimony / Occam’s Razor is essential for LLMs to be utilized in areas such as scientific discovery, which require inductive and abductive reasoning, yet this is often overlooked. Physicists, for example, strive for the simplest explanations to account for as many observations as possible. According to our findings, LLMs struggle to generate high-quality hypotheses that match human-level intelligence. To the best of our knowledge, prior studies of inductive and abductive reasoning did not consider parsimony / Occam’s Razor in their evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The high-level research question we want to answer in this paper is: <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Do LLMs reason inductively and abductively? If so, how well?</em> There is a significant body of work on evaluating the deductive reasoning capabilities of LLMs  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov and He, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tafjord et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, as well as techniques to improve deductive reasoning capabilities, such as in-context learning and reinforcement learning with verifiable reward, <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RLVR</em>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, there is relatively little exploration into the inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). This work systematically evaluates whether and to what extent LLMs can reason abductively and inductively. We also assess whether popular reasoning-enhancing techniques, such as in-context learning  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and RLVR  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which primarily aim to improve deductive reasoning capabilities, can also benefit inductive and abductive reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.03345v1/banner.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="152.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 584.11 152.21" width="584.11"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,152.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(246.76,0) translate(0,75.95)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -242.15 19.45)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.56em;--ltx-fo-depth:3.37em;font-size:10pt;" height="54.27" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="484.3"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:35em;">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#FBC15E;"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q:</span> All mammals are warm-blooded. Sam is a mammal. Tom is a mammal. John is a mammal. Each feline is a mammal. All canidae are mammals. All canidae are muscular. Every dog is a canidae. All wolves are canidae. All felines are not slow.
Each cat is a feline.
All tigers are felines. All cats are cute.
Alice is a cat. All dogs are smart. Bob is a dog. All wolves are gregarious. Jessica is a wolf. All tigers are strong. Susan is a tiger. All rodents are gnawing. Each rat is a rodent. Each squirrel is a rodent. All rats are adaptable. Jack is a rat. Noah is a rat. All squirrels are bushy. Oliver is a squirrel.</span><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;"> <span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#71ADD1;">We observe that Sam is hairy. Alice is hairy. Bob is hairy. Fae is strong. Fae is not slow. Fae is warm-blooded. Jack is a mammal. Noah is a mammal. Oliver is a mammal.</span><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Please produce hypotheses to explain all observations.<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-bg-color:#9AC155;"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A:</span> All mammals are hairy. Fae is a tiger. All rodents are mammals.</span></span> <span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.2.2.4" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 268.53 63.96)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.41em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:8.5pt;" height="7.69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="63.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;--ltx-bg-color:#FBC15E;">world model</span><span id="S1.F1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;"> </span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 268.53 -25.98)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.46em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:8.5pt;" height="7.69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="64.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;--ltx-bg-color:#71ADD1;">observations</span><span id="S1.F1.pic1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;"> </span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 268.53 -69.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.89em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:8.5pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="57.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;--ltx-bg-color:#9AC155;">hypotheses</span><span id="S1.F1.pic1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#4D4D4D;"> </span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#4D4D4D;" stroke="#4D4D4D"><path style="fill:none" d="M 263.64 67.8 L 247.04 67.8"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#4D4D4D;" stroke="#4D4D4D"><path style="fill:none" d="M 263.64 -22.14 L 247.04 -22.14"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#4D4D4D;" stroke="#4D4D4D"><path style="fill:none" d="M 263.64 -66.42 L 247.04 -66.42"></path></g></g></svg></span>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An example of a reasoning question in 
<span id="S1.F1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InAbHyD</span>
. The upper left side is the world model. Note that the dashed arrow means the subtype relationship <span id="S1.F1.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All rats are mammals</span> is hidden and needs to be inferred (i.e., a hypothesis to propose). Each node is a concept (e.g., <span id="S1.F1.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>), with its properties and members. Each edge denotes a subtype relationship (e.g., <span id="S1.F1.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are carnivores</span>). On the upper right-hand side are observations in this reasoning example and hypotheses (ground truth answers for this reasoning example). Each hypothesis explains observations of the same color (e.g., the hypothesis <span id="S1.F1.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All mammals are hairy</span> explains the observations <span id="S1.F1.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sam is hairy; Alice is hairy; Bob is hairy</span>). On the lower side is the natural language description of this reasoning example. Note that this example uses a real world model for illustrative purposes. In our dataset, we use fictional world models. A more detailed explanation of this example is given in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Detailed explanations of Figure ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We create a programmable and synthetic dataset, 
<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InAbHyD</span>
(<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In</span>ductive and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ab</span>ductive <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hy</span>pothesis <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>iscovery), to test LLMs’ inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities. Each reasoning example has a fictional <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incomplete</em> world model in the form of an ontology tree and a set of observations, represented in first-order logic. To answer each reasoning example, LLMs must produce hypotheses to explain observations under the incomplete world model. We generate synthetic examples of fictional world models to mitigate training data contamination. The ontology tree’s height characterizes the difficulty of the world model and the corresponding reasoning example.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          See Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an example and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Detailed explanations of Figure ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for more explanations for Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and more examples in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B More examples ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our dataset on five widely-used LLMs: <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama3-70B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite> , <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemma3-27B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamath et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-V3</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-4o</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023</a>)</cite> , and <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For each LLM, we test whether it can produce <em id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">valid</em> and <em id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-quality</em> hypotheses. We also test whether popular reasoning-enhancing techniques, such as in-context learning and RLVR, which primarily were meant to improve deductive reasoning, can also benefit inductive and abductive reasoning. Our findings can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs can perform inductive and abductive reasoning at high accuracy for shallow ontology trees with a single hypothesis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The accuracy drops significantly when the ontology tree structure is complex and there are multiple hypotheses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In some cases, LLMs may produce <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">valid</em> hypotheses (hypotheses which can explain all observations); but fail to produce <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-quality</em> hypotheses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, we observe that reasoning-enhancing techniques for deductive reasoning only <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">moderately</em> benefits inductive and abductive reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluating deductive reasoning capabilities.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is no consensus on whether LLMs can perform general deductive reasoning or are stochastic parrots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Researchers have designed different datasets and evaluation methodologies to answer this question  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov and He, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tafjord et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LogiQA</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite> is a multiple-choice QA dataset to test multiple types of deductive reasoning of language models. However, it only attends to label accuracy (<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the correctness of the final answer</em>) without considering the correctness of intermediate proof steps. In contrast, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov and He, 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA-OOD</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> evaluate both label accuracy and step-by-step proof correctness. They can synthetically generate proofs with different heights, widths, and deduction rules.
Our work is similar to <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA</span> and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ProntoQA-OOD</span> in spirit. We both use a
first-order ontology tree as the world model. However, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA</span> and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA-OOD</span> do not evaluate inductive and abductive reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluating non-deductive reasoning capabilities.</h5>

<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of existing datasets to evaluate inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities. <span id="S2.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FBF8E6;" alttext="\bm{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FBF8E6;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathbackground="#FBF8E6" mathvariant="bold">∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math></span> means not applicable.
</span></figcaption>
