<td id="S2.T1.6.11.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D7E9DB;padding:0.5pt 2.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.6.11.7.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:41.4pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.6.11.7.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.6.11.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-bg-color:#D7E9DB;">✓</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Evaluating non-deductive reasoning capabilities. ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the similarities and differences between our work and others on evaluating non-deductive reasoning capabilities in both the pre- and LLM eras  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Young et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hua et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Our work differs from others in the following ways. First, we focus on inductive and abductive reasoning and their interactions with deductive reasoning, while other works focus only on a single reasoning modality. Second, our reasoning problem is built upon a fictional world model with natural language descriptions. The fictional world model avoids training data contamination compared with ART  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where the problem is directly taken from a web corpus; the natural language descriptions of the reasoning problem render the evaluation more practical compared with symbolic reasoning, such as in InductiveBench  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hua et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Third, our dataset is programmable with highly-controllable difficulty parameters, allowing us to evaluate LLMs’ reasoning capabilities under various scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Techniques to enhance reasoning.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A large body of work has focused on improving the
(deductive) reasoning capabilities of LLMs and related downstream tasks such as mathematics and coding  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The first line of work aims to elicit the emergent capabilities of LLMs by different formats of prompts, such as chain-of-thought (CoT)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, tree-of-thought (ToT)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and self-consistency  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The second line of work leverages in-context learning by providing few-shot examples to LLMs. The third line of work views LLM token generation as a reinforcement learning problem and utilizes verifiable reward functions (reinforcement learning with verifiable reward, <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RLVR</em>; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al. 2025</a></cite>) to steer LLMs to improved reasoning.
The fourth line of work scales LLMs’ test time to increase the accuracy of reasoning problems. It is worth noting that most techniques discussed above focus mainly on deductive reasoning. This work investigates whether the above methods benefit inductive and abductive reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Approach</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe how we generate the dataset and evaluate LLMs on this dataset. At a high level, the dataset consists of synthetically-generated reasoning examples in natural language.
The question description is a user prompt, and the format specification is a system prompt to LLMs. We parse LLMs’ output to evaluate their inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities on metrics such as accuracy. We acknowledge that the potential space of inductive and abductive reasoning examples is vast, so we restrict ourselves to what is expressible in first-order logic. By composing the inductive and abductive reasoning steps in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3 Approach ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our evaluation framework can readily cover all inductive and abductive reasoning questions in first-order logic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each reasoning example contains a fictional world model and a set of observations. We use real person names (such as Amy) and real property names (such as strong), but we do not use real concept names (such as tiger). Instead, we use fictional concept names (such as wumpus). In doing so, LLMs will not be confused by these fictional relations, as we never generate facts that contradict those about real concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs must produce hypotheses to <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explain</em> the observations. Our <em id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hypothesize-explain</em> framework can cover a wide range of inductive/abductive reasoning scenarios.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          Traditionally, producing hypotheses to explain observations is considered abductive reasoning. However, we include inductive reasoning in the same framing. For example, given these axioms as a world model: <span id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is a cat;</span> <span id="footnote3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is a cat;</span> <span id="footnote3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span> and these observations: <span id="footnote3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is cute;</span> <span id="footnote3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is cute;</span> <span id="footnote3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is cute</span>, we can produce a hypothesis: <em id="footnote3.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</em> to explain all observations. This is <em id="footnote3.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inductive reasoning</em>.</span></span></span>
To generate a single reasoning example, we go through the following steps:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we construct a world model as an ontology tree in first-order logic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we traverse the ontology tree and randomly mark some axioms as hidden. Each hidden axiom is the ground truth for hypotheses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Third, for each hidden axiom, we generate observations so that LLMs have enough information to produce ground truth hypotheses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fourth, we translate the incomplete world model and observations into natural language.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:362.5pt;height:147.9pt;vertical-align:-72.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-15.8pt,6.4pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
