<span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_bottom"> <span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.T2.m8.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.14,1}o_{1}(A)}\mathstrut" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;" mathcolor="#8C24FF">o</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;" mathcolor="#8C24FF">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;" mathcolor="#8C24FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;" mathcolor="#8C24FF">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;" mathcolor="#8C24FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.14,1}o_{1}(A)}\mathstrut</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.T2.m8.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 8.00003pt{\color[rgb]{0,0.6,0}\forall x(o_{1}(x)\to o_{2}(x))}\mathstrut" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">o</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">o</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" mathcolor="#009900" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 8.00003pt{\color[rgb]{0,0.6,0}\forall x(o_{1}(x)\to o_{2}(x))}\mathstrut</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.4pt;"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:103.0pt;height:0.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_bottom">      <span id="S3.T2.3.1.4.6.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.T2.m8.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\color[rgb]{0,0,0}o_{2}(A)\mathstrut" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\color[rgb]{0,0,0}o_{2}(A)\mathstrut</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.36" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.37" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">An overview of various subtasks in 
<span id="S3.T2.37.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InAbHyD</span>
and their details in the simplest case (a height-1 ontology tree with a single hypothesis). Here <span id="S3.T2.37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o</span> denotes an ontology concept (e.g, <span id="S3.T2.37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>) and <span id="S3.T2.37.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o(A)</span> denotes the membership (e.g., <span id="S3.T2.37.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cat(Sally)</span> means <span id="S3.T2.37.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is a cat</span>). <span id="S3.T2.37.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">p</span> denotes a property (e.g, <span id="S3.T2.37.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cute</span>) and <span id="S3.T2.37.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">p(A)</span> denotes <span id="S3.T2.37.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A</span> has property <span id="S3.T2.37.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">p</span> (e.g., <span id="S3.T2.37.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cute(Sally)</span> means <span id="S3.T2.37.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is cute</span>). We only show the proof tree for one observation for simplicity; other observations can be derived similarly. We show hypotheses <span id="S3.T2.37.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">green</span> and axioms in world models <span id="S3.T2.37.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C24FF;">purple</span> in the proof tree.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">See Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3 Approach ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for an overview of the different subtasks and their details in the simplest case (a height-1 ontology tree with a single hypothesis).
We evaluate the output of LLMs (i.e., produced <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hypotheses</em>) on the following metrics:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Strong accuracy</em>: Hypotheses are strongly correct if they exactly match ground truth hypotheses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Weak accuracy</em>: Hypotheses are weakly correct if they explain all observations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hypothesis quality</em>: A quantitative measurement of the quality of the LLMs’ produced hypotheses based on the principles of parsimony.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we describe each step in more detail.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">World model generation</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our approach, the world model is an ontology tree in first-order logic. Each node in the ontology tree is a concept (e.g. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>) with properties (e.g. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>, or equivalently in logical form <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x(\texttt{cat}(x)\rightarrow\texttt{cute}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#0080FF">cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#0080FF">cute</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x(\texttt{cat}(x)\rightarrow\texttt{cute}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>) and members (e.g. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>, or equivalently in logical forms, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;"> <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cat(Amy)</span></span>). Each edge in the ontology tree denotes a subtype relationship (e.g., the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">ragdoll</span> node being a child of the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span> node implies <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Each ragdoll is a cat</span>, or equivalently in logical form, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;"> <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x(\texttt{ragdoll}(x)\rightarrow\texttt{cat}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#0080FF">ragdoll</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#0080FF">cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;" mathcolor="#0080FF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x(\texttt{ragdoll}(x)\rightarrow\texttt{cat}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></span>).
We first generate the topology of the ontology tree with the height as a controllable parameter. Each non-leaf node has two or three child nodes. The height of the ontology tree is a variable that characterizes the complexity of the world model. We then make two passes of the tree in level order traversal. In the first pass, for each node, we allocate a concept name, three properties,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            The properties can also be negated properties, e.g., <span id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are not slow</span>.</span></span></span> and three members.
After the first pass, we have a <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complete</em> world model with three types of axioms:

<span id="S3.I3" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S3.I3.i1.2" class="ltx_text">properties (e.g., <span id="S3.I3.i1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>),
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S3.I3.i2.2" class="ltx_text">membership relations (e.g., <span id="S3.I3.i2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>),
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="S3.I3.i3.2" class="ltx_text">subtype relations (e.g., <span id="S3.I3.i3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Each ragdoll is a cat</span>).
</span></span>
</span>
In the second pass, we randomly mark some axioms as hidden. These axioms are the ground truth hypotheses. After the second pass, we have an <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incomplete</em> world model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Observation generation</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we generate observations so that LLMs have sufficient information to produce the ground truth hypotheses. The observations must be complex enough that the reasoning example is non-trivial, yet they should also ensure the
reasoning is feasible.</p>
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate an example where the task is to infer a property (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>), we first identify the type in the ontology with this property (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>), then we sample members of this type and subtypes (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is a ragdoll</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is a shorthair</span>), and add observations where the members have this property (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is cute</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is cute</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is cute</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate an example where the task is to infer a membership relation (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>), we first identify the type in the ontology with this member (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>), then we sample properties of this type and supertypes (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All felines are not slow</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All mammals are hairy</span>), and add observations where this member have these properties (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is cute</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is not slow</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is hairy</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate an example where the task is to infer a subtype relation (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are felines</span>), we first identify the subtype in the ontology (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cat</span>), then we sample members of this type and its subtypes (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is a ragdoll</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is a shorthair</span>), and add observations where these members belong to the supertype (e.g., <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a feline</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Polly is a feline</span>; <span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Sally is a feline</span>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Translate theories and observations into natural language</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same grammar as in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrOntoQA</span> for translating logical forms into natural language. Properties are translated into the form:
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>; subtype relations are randomly translated into one of three forms: <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Each ragdoll is a cat;</span> <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Every ragdoll is a cat;</span> <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All ragdolls are cats</span>; membership relations are translated into the form <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a cat</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hypothesis quality</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a metric to evaluate the quality of hypotheses
based on the principles of parsimony, or Occam’s Razor (i.e., the priciple that, other things being equal, simpler theories are better;  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker 2022</a></cite>).
In this regard, we encourage one hypothesis to explain as many observations as possible and penalize unnecessary hypotheses.
Formally, given a world model <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, a set of observations <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, their associated proof trees <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>,candidate hypotheses <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and ground truth hypotheses <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(h)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of times <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> appears in the proof trees of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., how many times <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is used as a premise of a proof step). We use <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{H}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{H}|</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the cardinality of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quality of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            We write the equation as the ratio of two averages: the numerator is the average accuracy of the predicted hypothesis (i.e., the average number of times a hypothesis appears in any valid proof), and the denominator is the average number of times a hypothesis appears in the shortest (i.e., most parsimonious) proof.</span></span></span>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathcal{H})=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mathcal{H}\ \text{cannot explain}\ \mathcal{O}\\
\frac{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}n(h)}{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}^{*}|}\sum_{h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}}n(h^{*})}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>cannot explain</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathcal{H})=\begin{cases}0&amp;\mathcal{H}\ \text{cannot explain}\ \mathcal{O}\\
\frac{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}n(h)}{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}^{*}|}\sum_{h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}}n(h^{*})}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">According to the definition, the ground truth hypotheses <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> have a quality score of 1. Consider the example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, here <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is {<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Fae is a tiger</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All mammals are hairy</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All rodents are mammals</span>} and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{H}^{*}|=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{H}^{*}|=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> appears three times in the proof trees of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}}n(h^{*})=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{h^{*}\in\mathcal{H}^{*}}n(h^{*})=9</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider candidate hypotheses <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> {<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Fae is a tiger</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All mammals are hairy</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All rats are mammals</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All squirrels are mammals</span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are hairy</span>}. <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{H}|=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{H}|=5</annotation></semantics></math>. Obviously <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> can explain <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All rats are mammals</span> appears twice, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All squirrels are mammals</span> appears once, and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are hairy</span> appear once in the proof trees of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}n(h)=3+3+2+1+1=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{h\in\mathcal{H}}n(h)=3+3+2+1+1=10</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathcal{H})=\frac{3\times 10}{5\times 9}=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathcal{H})=\frac{3\times 10}{5\times 9}=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Algorithms ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> contains the complete algorithms for generating reasoning examples and evaluating hypotheses.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In each experiment configuration, we generate 100 independent and identically distributed reasoning examples. Each reasoning example is pure text, a user prompt with an incomplete world model and observations. We also specify the desired output format in the system prompt. To answer each reasoning example, LLMs must produce hypotheses to explain all observations. We evaluate hypotheses on three metrics: weak accuracy, strong accuracy, and quality. We compute 95% confidence intervals for both accuracy metrics using the Wilson interval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilson, 1927</a>)</cite>.
We use the OpenAI API to evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-4o</span> and <a href="https://www.together.ai/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">together.ai</a> platform to evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama3-70B</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemma3-27B</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-V3</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we vary the following variables: 
<span id="S4.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">Different types of tasks (i.e., infer property, infer membership relation, or infer subtype relation),
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">Single hypothesis or multiple hypotheses,
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text">The height of the ontology tree,
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(4)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i4.2" class="ltx_text">Whether the reasoning example contains in-context demonstrations and whether they are in-distribution or out-of-distribution.</span></span>
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Zero-shot results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To begin evaluating the inductive and abductive reasoning abilities of LLMs, we first investigate the simplest setting with zero-shot prompting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Single hypothesis</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first investigate results for reasoning examples with one hypothesis for three tasks. This helps us to isolate different types of tasks and understand how LLMs perform in each case. The results are in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2.1 Single hypothesis ‣ 4.2 Zero-shot results ‣ 4 Results ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In general, all models show high accuracy (both weak and strong) greater than 80%
for all three tasks under a height-1 ontology tree. When the height of the ontology tree starts to increase, we also observe a general decreasing trend in all three metrics, which is not surprising. One interesting observation is that LLMs perform better in <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer membership relation</em> tasks than other tasks, especially for <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemma3-27B</span>. For example, even under a height-4 ontology tree, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gemma3-27B</span> can achieve near 50% weak accuracy, while the number drops to around 10% for <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer subtype relation</em> tasks. This may be because the weak correctness for <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer membership relation</em> tasks only requires searching for the concept that has some property, but do not need to understand the structure of the ontology tree.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
              e.g., given <span id="footnote6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is cute</span>, LLMs only need to search for a concept which has the property <span id="footnote6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">cute</span> to infer the membership relation of <span id="footnote6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy</span>.</span></span></span> However, this is not the case for <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer subtype relation</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer properties</em> tasks. We observe a similar trend for <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer subtype relation</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infer properties</em> tasks, as we can view the concept’s parent as a special property of that concept.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
