<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Weak accuracy, strong accuracy, and quality versus the height of the ontology tree comparison results for <span id="S4.F4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama3-70B</span> and <span id="S4.F4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span> with multiple hypotheses.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RLVR has demonstrated significant improvements in other reasoning benchmarks, and so we test whether this improvement generalizes to abductive and inductive reasoning. Specifically, we compare <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama3-70B</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span>. In general, we observe an average performance improvement between 10% and 20%. To analyze why <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span> performs better than <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama3-70B</span>, we also manually inspect the reasoning output of <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span> (i.e., tokens that are inside <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/think&gt;</span>). After producing hypotheses, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-70B</span> will usually attempt to verify those hypotheses. This verification process is essentially a deductive reasoning process, and can catch some reasoning errors or fix low-quality hypotheses. A complete example is in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E LLM example responses ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Analysis of common errors</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further understand <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> and <em id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> LLMs fail, we manually inspect mistaken and low-quality hypotheses. We summarize some of the most common types of errors here for further insights. We provide example responses in each category in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E LLM example responses ‣ Language Models Do Not Follow Occam’s Razor: A Benchmark for Inductive and Abductive Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.

<span id="S4.I3" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S4.I3.i1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wrong ontology direction.</span><span id="S4.I3.i1.3" class="ltx_text">LLMs sometimes produce hypotheses in the wrong direction. For example, the ground truth hypothesis is <span id="S4.I3.i1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are mammals</span>, while LLMs will produce the hypothesis <span id="S4.I3.i1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All mammals are cats</span>.
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S4.I3.i2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ignore the ontology and produce unnecessary hypotheses.</span><span id="S4.I3.i2.3" class="ltx_text">LLMs sometimes can produce unnecessary hypotheses because they ignore the ontology. For example, the ground truth hypothesis is <span id="S4.I3.i2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute</span>, while LLMs produce two hypotheses: <span id="S4.I3.i2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">All cats are cute; All ragdolls are cute</span>.
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="S4.I3.i3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fall back to trivial hypotheses.</span><span id="S4.I3.i3.3" class="ltx_text">Technically, the observation is also a valid hypothesis, but it is considered low-quality because it can only explain itself. LLMs sometimes reuse observations as hypotheses.
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(4)</span> <span id="S4.I3.i4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucinated entities.</span><span id="S4.I3.i4.3" class="ltx_text">LLMs sometimes produce hypotheses with hallucinated entities (including concepts, properties, members). Usually, this happens when the entity appears in the in-context demonstrations, but LLMs use them in the test question.
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I3.i5" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(5)</span> <span id="S4.I3.i5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confuse members with concepts.</span><span id="S4.I3.i5.3" class="ltx_text">We use fictional names for concepts and real-person names for members. However, LLMs sometimes do confuse these two. For example, the ground truth hypothesis is <span id="S4.I3.i5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Amy is a wumpus</span>, while LLMs produce <span id="S4.I3.i5.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Wumpus is Amy</span>.</span></span>
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions and future work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we introduced a programmable and synthetic dataset, 
<span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InAbHyD</span>
. Our dataset serves as an out-of-distribution evaluation of the inductive and abductive reasoning abilities of LLMs. We do not claim that success in our benchmark automatically implies success in real-world tasks, but rather, our benchmark serves as a checkpoint for developing models that are capable of robust real-world abductive and inductive reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We systematically studied LLMs’ inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities. Our results suggest that current LLMs can’t generate high-quality hypotheses that match human-level intelligence. For example, suppose we use LLMs as a scientific discovery assistant. In that case, they may generate superficial hypotheses that can explain observations but fail to uncover more general hypotheses that reveal hidden structures or connections in science. Our results can serve to better inform practitioners of LLMs of their limitations, especially in applications that rely heavily on inductive and abductive reasoning. The dataset itself opens new avenues for future work, such as training or fine-tuning models to improve their inductive and abductive reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One limitation of this work is that first-order logic (FOL) limits the expressiveness of the world model and the reasoning example. We leave it as a future work to use higher-order logic (HOL) to generate more complex world models and reasoning examples, which would require translating from HOL into natural language and vice versa. The other crucial future direction is improving the quality of LLMs’ hypotheses. For that, LLMs may benefit from RLVR  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that explicitly conveys principles of parsimony in inductive and abductive reasoning with an appropriate reward. Another interesting future direction will be to determine whether large language models fine-tuned on this synthetic dataset (using supervised fine-tuning or reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards) could improve their performance on real-world tasks involving inductive and abductive reasoning. If this is the case, this synthetic dataset is more valuable because we can essentially generate an infinite number of synthetic data points, without worrying about the bottleneck of sampling data from the real world.</p>
</div>
</section>
